export const complianceFaq=[
 ['What is crypto compliance software?','It supports one or more tasks such as identity checks, sanctions screening, blockchain monitoring, Travel Rule data exchange, investigations, case management or tax reporting. These are different functions, so choose a stack against your business activity and documented obligations.'],
 ['Which crypto compliance provider is best?','There is no universal winner. Compare the exact workflow, assets, jurisdictions, evidence, integration and operating responsibilities. A provider that fits an investigation team may not solve a merchant onboarding or tax-reporting problem.'],
 ['What is the difference between KYC, KYT and the Travel Rule?','KYC concerns customer identity and due diligence. KYT tools help assess transaction activity and exposure. Travel Rule workflows exchange required originator and beneficiary information where applicable. Passing one step does not complete the others.'],
 ['Are wallet screening and transaction monitoring the same?','No. Screening assesses an address or transaction at a point in time. Monitoring can reassess activity or exposure over time and generate alerts. Specify when screening occurs, what is monitored and who reviews changes.'],
 ['Does a low wallet risk score prove that funds are safe?','No. Scores depend on available intelligence, attribution, coverage and configuration. Consider customer and transaction context, inspect the evidence and retain an accountable decision. A lack of a known label does not prove the absence of risk.'],
 ['Can one provider cover the entire compliance process?','Some suppliers offer multiple modules and partner integrations. Verify each required function and its data flows. One interface does not prove that every module has identical coverage or that operational and legal responsibilities have transferred to the supplier.'],
 ['How should we compare blockchain coverage?','List the exact network, asset contract, transaction type and required feature. Test screening, tracing, monitoring and exports separately. A token count, a chain count and investigation depth are different measures.'],
 ['Does Travel Rule message delivery mean the payment is approved?','No. Message validation, counterparty acceptance, your policy decision and blockchain settlement are separate events. Test incomplete data, a rejected message, an unavailable counterparty and a transfer that arrives before the information.'],
 ['Can blockchain intelligence identify a wallet owner conclusively?','Not by itself. Attribution may be based on different sources or analytical methods. Ask for provenance and distinguish an address label, service cluster, contracting entity and beneficial owner before relying on the result.'],
 ['Do compliance tools automatically file every required report?','No. Confirm the jurisdiction, form, reporting entity, validation, approval and actual submission integration. A generated narrative or downloadable file is not proof of successful filing or regulator acceptance.'],
 ['Are all 29 directory records independent companies?','No. The list includes company and product records. Elliptic Lens is an Elliptic product, Chainalysis VASP Risking is a Chainalysis product, and Coinfirm is part of Lukka. The Civic identity reference is kept separate from current AML-tool comparisons.'],
 ['Does buying compliance software make a business licensed?','No. Software supports controls but does not grant authorization, remove obligations or guarantee regulatory acceptance. Identify the applicable entities, activities and jurisdictions with qualified advisers.'],
 ['How can we test a provider without exposing customer data?','Start with a documented sandbox and synthetic or properly authorized test data. Agree security, access, retention and transfer terms before using personal information. Never place identity documents or confidential case details in public URLs or analytics.'],
 ['How can FluidRWA help us shortlist providers?','Share the business activity, jurisdictions, assets, workflows and integration you need. FluidRWA can help organize a requirements-led shortlist; your team retains responsibility for technical, commercial, legal and compliance diligence.']
];
const section=(id,kicker,title,body)=>`<section class="cc-section bc-section" id="${id}"><div class="light-container"><div class="cc-heading"><p class="eyebrow light-eyebrow">${kicker}</p><h2>${title}</h2></div>${body}</div></section>`;
const table=(caption,head,rows)=>`<div class="cc-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="${esc(caption)}"><table><caption>${caption}</caption><thead><tr>${head.map(h=>`<th scope="col">${h}</th>`).join('')}</tr></thead><tbody>${rows.map(row=>`<tr>${row.map((v,i)=>`<${i?'td':'th scope="row"'}>${v}</${i?'td':'th'}>`).join('')}</tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>`;
const guide=()=>[
section('compliance-workflows','Start with the decision','A flagged transfer is the beginning of the work',`<p>A customer has passed onboarding. Their deposit arrives, a wallet tool raises an alert, and the operations team cannot explain why the balance is unavailable. The customer wants an answer, finance needs the transaction reconciled, and compliance needs evidence before deciding what happens next. Another dashboard will not fix the handoff unless the responsibilities are clear.</p><p>Crypto compliance infrastructure connects identity, transaction intelligence, counterparty information, policy and case handling. The useful question is not simply which platform has the most features. It is whether your team can make and defend a decision across the complete customer journey, including an exception, a correction and a later review.</p>${table('Match the control to the job',['Job','What the tool contributes','What your team must still decide'],[
 ['Customer and business onboarding','Document or data checks, beneficial-owner information, identity and business risk signals.','Eligibility, required due diligence, review escalation and whether to establish the relationship.'],
 ['Sanctions, PEP and adverse-media screening','Possible name/entity matches, identifiers, lists and supporting sources.','Whether it is the same party, which measures apply and the appropriate action.'],
 ['Wallet and transaction risk','On-chain exposure, labels, risk categories, monitoring alerts and tracing.','How the evidence relates to this customer and transaction, and whether to hold or escalate.'],
 ['Travel Rule communication','Counterparty discovery, required data collection, validation and secure exchange.','Applicable rules, adequate information, counterparty acceptance and transfer policy.'],
 ['Investigation and case operations','Evidence collection, assignments, notes, approval and reporting workflow.','The supported conclusion, accountable reviewer and any reporting or escalation.'],
 ['Market integrity','Order/trade surveillance and patterns associated with market abuse.','Which activity needs investigation and the response appropriate to the operated venue.'],
 ['Tax and accounting','Transaction classification, reconciliation, tax documentation and reporting outputs.','Applicable accounting/tax treatment, reporting entity, approvals and corrections.']
 ])}<p>A smart-contract audit addresses code and deployment scope. It does not replace AML monitoring. A tax report is not a sanctions decision. A verified identity does not prove control of every wallet a customer supplies. Keep these boundaries in the requirements before choosing suppliers.</p>`),
section('compliance-compare','Compare like with like','Build the stack around the business you operate',`<p>An exchange, custodian, stablecoin payments business and tokenized-asset issuer may all buy compliance tools, but their data, counterparties and decision points differ. Start with legal entities, business activities, customer types, operating jurisdictions and the products you actually provide. Have qualified advisers identify the requirements for that scope; software marketing is not a legal assessment.</p>${table('Illustrative shortlisting paths',['Business need','Relevant functions','Demonstration to request'],[
 ['Exchange or custodian','Onboarding, screening, deposits/withdrawals, counterparty checks, Travel Rule and cases.','A deposit held for review, a withdrawal stopped before broadcast and a resolved case with retained evidence.'],
 ['Stablecoin payments or fintech','Customer/business checks, wallet and payment risk, beneficiary data, Travel Rule and bank-partner handoffs.','A complete payer-to-beneficiary route, including missing information and a returned payment.'],
 ['Tokenized-asset platform','Investor onboarding, beneficial owners, eligibility, wallet linkage and transfer-control integration.','An investor change, wallet replacement and restricted transfer without losing the audit trail.'],
 ['Investigation team','Cross-chain tracing, attribution evidence, collaboration and reproducible export.','A known test path with bridges, swaps and uncertain attribution clearly distinguished.'],
 ['Finance or tax team','Complete ingestion, transfers, accounting classification, reconciliation and reporting.','A period close with missing history, a corrected transaction and an acknowledged reporting output.']
 ])}<p>Chainalysis, Elliptic, TRM Labs, Crystal Intelligence, Merkle Science and Scorechain are relevant starting points for on-chain analytics. Notabene, Sygna and VerifyVASP describe Travel Rule workflows. Sumsub spans several onboarding and transaction modules. ComplyAdvantage and LSEG World-Check bring screening data and workflows, while Hummingbird and Flagright are relevant to operational case handling. Compare the exact products, not an undifferentiated best-vendor list.</p><p>Existing customers may benefit from a multi-module supplier, but consolidation can increase dependency on one data source or one operating system. Ask which components are native, supplied by a partner, separately licensed or dependent on information your business must provide. One contract and one interface do not automatically mean one accountable end-to-end service.</p>`),
section('compliance-coverage','Evidence before a score','Test coverage at the asset and feature level',`<p>Build a coverage sheet with network, chain identifier, asset contract, transaction type and the feature you need. An address lookup, token transfer history, continuous monitoring and cross-chain investigation are different capabilities. A large token count can describe many contracts on a small number of networks, while a large network count may include read-only or limited support.</p><h3>Ask what supports the conclusion</h3><p>For each result, request the risk category, source or attribution basis, relevant path, amount and timing, and when the intelligence was updated. Separate a directly designated address from indirect exposure, a service-level cluster from an individual account, and an allegation from a confirmed fact. Ask how a team can challenge a label and how corrections reach previously generated alerts.</p><p>A numeric score is a model output, not a universal measurement that can be compared across vendors without understanding the inputs. Two providers can assign different scores to the same address because their labels, propagation rules, risk weights and observation windows differ. Compare the explanation and the outcome on your approved cases rather than averaging incompatible numbers.</p><h3>Depth matters more than a chain total</h3><p>Include account-based and UTXO transactions where relevant, token transfers, contract calls, exchange deposit addresses, bridges, swaps, mixers and protocol interactions. Confirm whether internal transfers or omnibus addresses limit visibility. A supported bridge name does not prove that the tool reconstructs every route or recognizes the beneficiary after a swap. A missing label is not proof of low risk.</p><p>Retain the original input, returned result, timestamp, product version where available, risk-policy version and the reason for the action. Intelligence can change after a decision. An analyst reviewing the case later needs to distinguish what was known then from what was learned afterwards, without rewriting the historical record.</p><h3>Use a controlled comparison set</h3><p>Create representative examples with permission to use the data: expected matches, legitimate near-matches, clean business activity, indirect exposure, known historical incidents and ambiguous cases. Record the expected review path, not just a preferred color. Keep a separate set for final acceptance so configuration is not tuned only to familiar examples.</p>`),
section('compliance-screening','Customer context','Name screening and wallet intelligence need different evidence',`<p>A sanctions or PEP name match requires enough identifiers to distinguish the customer from another person. Test dates of birth, country information, corporate identifiers, aliases and transliteration where relevant. Missing information should trigger an appropriate review, not a fabricated match confidence. Politically exposed person status, adverse media and sanctions designation are different categories with different implications.</p><p>Review source relevance, publication and event dates, language handling, duplicate articles and the ability to record why a match was dismissed. A case about a historic allegation should not silently become a conclusion about current misconduct. Configure ongoing screening and changes in customer information so the original onboarding decision is not treated as permanently sufficient.</p><h3>Protect legitimate users from avoidable friction</h3><p>Evaluate completion by the customer and document types you serve. Include mobile devices, interrupted uploads, accessible alternatives and a manual-review path. Keep a clear message about what information is needed and what happens next. Repeatedly collecting the same document without explaining the failure creates friction without necessarily improving assurance.</p><p>For businesses, separate legal-entity identification, ownership structure, beneficial-owner checks and authorized signatories. Ask which sources are checked and who resolves complex or inconsistent ownership information. A company registry record or a successful identity-document check is an input, not the entire customer risk assessment.</p><p>Wallet control also needs its own method and scope. A signed message may demonstrate control of a key under specific conditions; it does not alone establish source of funds, beneficial ownership or permitted use. Treat exchange deposit addresses, smart-contract wallets and self-hosted wallets according to the actual relationship and applicable requirements.</p>`),
section('compliance-travel','Communication is not settlement','Design for Travel Rule exceptions before launch',`<p>The Travel Rule concerns information accompanying certain transfers under applicable requirements. It is not a single global software protocol or one universal threshold. Determine the rules for the relevant entities, activities and jurisdictions before configuring fields and decision logic. FATF standards, local implementation and the requirements of a receiving counterparty are related but not identical.</p><p>The EBA's final Travel Rule Guidelines under Regulation (EU) 2023/1113 specify detection and handling of missing or incomplete information, with an application date of December 30, 2024. Use the actual source and professional advice for your deployment rather than importing a generic threshold from a comparison article.</p><h3>Test a bilateral journey, not just an API response</h3><p>Map originator and beneficiary fields, counterparty identification, wallet or account references, message status and the linked transfer. Check the actual legal entity behind the receiving service. A recognized brand or a network member record does not establish that the destination address belongs to the intended entity or that every customer can use that route.</p>${table('Travel Rule exception tests',['Situation','What to demonstrate','Evidence to retain'],[
 ['Missing or malformed information','Validation, information request, review ownership and a controlled hold or other appropriate action.','Original message, validation result, correction and decision.'],
 ['Unavailable or unsupported counterparty','Discovery attempts, interoperability limits and an approved exception process.','Counterparty identity, attempted route, status and escalation.'],
 ['Receiving provider rejects the message','Reason handling, correction and prevention of duplicate transfers.','Message versions and the final response.'],
 ['Transfer arrives before information','Linking, restricted availability where appropriate and subsequent review.','On-chain reference, information arrival time and action.'],
 ['Self-hosted wallet','A workflow suited to the applicable requirements and evidence of the relationship or control where needed.','Method, inputs, outcome and reviewer rationale.'],
 ['Name or wallet changes during review','Revalidation without silently attaching old information to a new transfer.','Version history and approved final instruction.']
 ])}<p>Exchange personal data through an appropriately secured channel. Do not put identity details, home addresses or sensitive case narratives on a public blockchain, in public URLs or in analytics events. Confirm encryption, access, retention, subprocessors, cross-border arrangements and incident obligations before sending live customer information.</p><p>Successful transmission, recipient validation, internal approval, blockchain broadcast and financial settlement should have distinct states. If the message succeeds but the transfer fails, operations needs a linked exception. If funds move but the message remains unresolved, compliance needs an escalation path rather than an automatic completed label.</p>`),
section('compliance-cases','Make alerts actionable','An alert needs an owner, evidence and a defensible outcome',`<p>Define who reviews each alert, who can request information, who can restrict activity and who approves a final disposition. Agree priority, service windows, escalation and backup ownership. A real-time alert has limited value if it waits in an unassigned queue while withdrawals remain enabled.</p><h3>Keep screening, holding and execution separate</h3><p>Specify the decision point in the money movement. A pre-withdrawal check can inform whether to broadcast; a deposit alert arrives after funds are already received; ongoing rescreening can change the risk assessment of a historic counterparty. The same risk signal may therefore require different operational handling. Tools do not undo a finalized blockchain transfer.</p><p>Cases should link customer information, transaction references, intelligence, communications, policy versions and analyst reasoning. Record the review action, approver and timestamps. Support reopening a case when new information arrives while preserving the earlier decision. An export that contains only the final status is inadequate for understanding how the decision was made.</p><h3>Distinguish a draft report from a filed report</h3><p>For regulatory reporting, verify the actual jurisdiction, form, required data and reporting entity. Test validation, reviewer sign-off, transmission, acknowledgements and correction handling. A PDF, narrative or generated file is not proof of submission or acceptance. Keep applicable reporting deadlines and confidentiality controls in the operating procedure, with qualified advice for the requirements.</p><p>Tax, accounting and suspicious-activity reports have different purposes, recipients and approval requirements. Do not reuse one output as evidence that another obligation was fulfilled. Finance may need reconciled economic activity, while investigators need risk evidence and a compliance officer may need a confidential reporting decision.</p><h3>Use automation with controlled authority</h3><p>Automation can gather evidence, prioritize work and suggest a narrative. Test whether it separates source facts from inference, links assertions to underlying records and handles contradictory inputs. Define which actions require human approval. Keep tool permissions narrow and prevent an automated summary from changing payment policy or releasing a held balance without authorization.</p><p>For generated outputs, test source omission, mistaken identity, unsupported certainty and instructions embedded in uploaded documents or external data. A model-assisted case narrative is still a draft that must be checked. Record relevant configuration and reviewer changes so later quality assurance can reproduce the reasoning and identify recurring failures.</p>`),
section('compliance-integration','From dashboard to production','Make the control reliable when a dependency fails',`<p>Use scoped credentials, approved environments and signed or otherwise authenticated callbacks according to the supplier's documentation. Test timeouts, rate limits, pagination, retries and duplicated or out-of-order notifications. Link each lookup, alert and case to a durable internal reference. A vendor error must not be mistaken for a clean result.</p><h3>Choose failure behavior explicitly</h3><p>Agree what happens when screening is unavailable, a network is unsupported, evidence is incomplete or a service takes too long. Your policy may call for holding, declining, restricting a feature or manual review depending on the context. Never silently treat an unavailable check as approval. Record the exception so pending work can be reconciled after service resumes.</p><p>Define data freshness for lists, customer records and blockchain intelligence separately. Ask about ingestion delays, stale caches, reorganization handling and historical backfill. A fast API response can return stale data; a delayed webhook can still refer to a completed scan. Make timestamps meaningful to the application and to the analyst.</p><h3>Security and privacy are procurement requirements</h3><p>Review data flow, access controls, tenant separation, encryption, audit logs, retention and deletion against your needs. Request the actual scope and period of security assurance reports rather than treating a certification logo as a guarantee about every product. Agree breach notification, subprocessor changes, backups and access when a supplier relationship ends.</p><p>Public blockchain data can become sensitive when linked to a known customer, personal identifier or investigation. Do not assume that on-chain visibility makes all combined data non-personal. Keep test environments separate, minimize data, and ensure exports and support tickets follow the same access controls as the case system.</p><h3>Plan a controlled exit</h3><p>Before signing, confirm the right to retain case evidence, exported labels or reports, and decision logs after termination. Test readable exports, identifiers, attachments and policy versions. A second supplier may use different scores, so migration should preserve the original rationale rather than overwrite it with a new number. Reconcile open alerts and unresolved transfer states before switching integrations.</p>`),
section('compliance-pilot','A useful acceptance test','Measure decisions and operations, not just alert volume',`<p>Write acceptance criteria before tuning the system. Agree the representative data, review labels, success criteria and limitations. A historical test can show whether known cases are detected; it cannot prove future detection or guarantee that unknown misconduct is absent. A sandbox validates integration behavior, not every production dependency.</p>${table('Pilot scorecard',['Measure','Useful definition','Common trap'],[
 ['Coverage','Required asset/feature combinations that work in the agreed environment.','Comparing advertised chain totals.'],
 ['Detection on labeled examples','Expected review cases detected, with the test-set assumptions recorded.','Claiming detection of all crime from a selected sample.'],
 ['False-positive burden','Legitimate examples incorrectly escalated, plus review effort.','Reducing alerts by weakening rules without testing missed cases.'],
 ['Time to outcome','End-to-end time through checking, queueing, review and authorized action.','Reporting only the fastest API response.'],
 ['Case completeness','Required evidence and approval present in a reproducible sample.','Counting a closed status as a well-supported decision.'],
 ['Interoperability','Important bilateral message journeys that complete, including exceptions.','Equating network membership with successful data exchange.'],
 ['Resilience','Unavailable-check, timeout, replay and backlog scenarios handled under policy.','Treating service uptime as proof that every control ran.'],
 ['Exit readiness','Usable export and reconciliation of open obligations.','Discovering missing attachments after termination.']
 ])}<p>Record observation period, sample size, exclusions and who labeled the examples. Precision and recall only have meaning against a defined reference set. Results from one customer population or chain should not be presented as the vendor's universal accuracy. Review both the operational workload and the cases the system missed.</p><p>Ask references about a comparable business, asset mix and deployment. A published customer logo, testimonial or case study is evidence of a relationship or selected experience, not a forecast for your team. Funding raised, awards and market visibility also do not establish control effectiveness. Use them as background, not the acceptance criterion.</p>`),
section('compliance-official','Official reference points','Keep implementation dates separate from marketing claims',`<p>Use primary materials for the obligations your advisers assess. The following are reference points, not a legal determination about your business or a vendor approval list:</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Fatfrecommendations/targeted-updated-virtualassets-vasps-2026.html">FATF's seventh virtual-assets targeted update</a>, published July 16, 2026, discusses implementation progress and gaps. A global standard and a jurisdiction's implemented law should not be treated as identical.</li><li><a href="https://www.eba.europa.eu/activities/single-rulebook/regulatory-activities/anti-money-laundering-and-countering-financing-terrorism/guidelines-information-requirements-relation-transfers-funds-and-certain-crypto-assets-transfers">EBA Travel Rule Guidelines</a> identify missing-information procedures and state an application date of December 30, 2024. Confirm the requirements for your entity and transfer, including self-hosted-address cases where relevant.</li><li><a href="https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20211015">OFAC's virtual-currency industry guidance</a> was issued October 15, 2021. Use current sanctions resources and appropriate advice alongside the guidance; its publication date is not today's list-update date.</li></ul><p>A tool that supports a control does not grant a licence, certify an entire AML programme or guarantee regulator acceptance. Confirm the exact legal entity, activities, customers and jurisdictional requirements. Keep dated source documents and the reasoning behind configuration changes rather than assuming a supplier will update every policy automatically.</p>`),
section('compliance-rfp','Your procurement brief','Give each provider the same concrete questions',`<p>Describe the operating problem, not just a request for an all-in-one platform. State the activity, entities, jurisdictions, customer types, assets, networks, data sources, expected workload and the systems that will consume the decision. Keep personal information out of the initial brief.</p><ol><li>Which exact products, data sources and partner modules are included?</li><li>Which asset, network, transaction and feature combinations meet our requirements?</li><li>What evidence supports labels, exposure paths and risk scores?</li><li>How do analysts challenge attribution and receive later corrections?</li><li>Which customer, business and wallet-control checks remain our responsibility?</li><li>How do you connect to our important Travel Rule counterparties?</li><li>What happens with missing information, rejected messages or unavailable checks?</li><li>Who can hold, release or escalate activity, and where is that authority recorded?</li><li>Can we version rules, test changes and roll back a configuration?</li><li>What data reaches case management and the final reviewer?</li><li>Which reporting outputs are drafts, files or actual submissions?</li><li>How are approvals, acknowledgements and corrections retained?</li><li>What security, privacy, access and retention terms apply to our deployment?</li><li>What support, incident response, training and implementation work are included?</li><li>How do we export evidence, reconcile open work and exit?</li></ol><p>Compare the commercial scope on the same basis: implementation, data, modules, users, monitored activity, storage, support and export rights. Record exclusions and dependencies in the agreement. Do not let a convenient dashboard conceal an unowned compliance decision or an integration that your team cannot maintain.</p><div class="cc-actions"><h3>Build a requirements-led shortlist</h3><p>Tell us the workflows, assets and jurisdictions your team needs to support.</p><a class="btn btn-primary light-primary" href="/submit-requirement?category=Compliance%20Infrastructure&source=compliance-directory">Share compliance requirements</a><a class="btn btn-soft" href="/downloads/crypto-compliance-buyer-checklist.txt" download>Download buyer checklist</a><a href="/apply">Apply to be a Vetted Vendor</a></div><p>For a focused onboarding comparison, see <a href="/vendors/kyc-aml-providers">KYC and AML providers</a>. Connect the control design to <a href="/vendors/crypto-custody-providers">custody operations</a>, <a href="/vendors/stablecoin-infrastructure-providers">stablecoin payments</a> and <a href="/vendors/crypto-accounting-tax-software">crypto accounting and tax software</a>. The <a href="/blog/chainalysis-vs-trm-vs-elliptic-blockchain-analytics">Chainalysis, TRM Labs and Elliptic comparison</a> provides a narrower analytics starting point.</p>`)
].join('');
export function enhanceComplianceCategory(html){
 const root=parse(html),old=new Map(root.querySelectorAll('.bc-provider-card,.bc-company-card').map(n=>[n.id,n]));
 const sorted=[...complianceRecords].sort((a,b)=>a[1].localeCompare(b[1],'en'));
 root.querySelector('title').set_content(complianceTitle);
 for(const [selector,value] of [['meta[name="description"]',complianceDescription],['meta[property="og:title"]',complianceTitle],['meta[property="og:description"]',complianceDescription],['meta[property="og:url"]',complianceUrl]])root.querySelector(selector)?.setAttribute('content',value);
 root.querySelector('link[rel="canonical"]').setAttribute('href',complianceUrl);
 const cards=sorted.map(([id,name,roles,description,question])=>{
  const previous=old.get(id);if(!previous)throw new Error(`Missing preserved record ${id}`);
  const mark=previous.querySelector('.bc-logo-mark,.bc-company-mark');
  const profile=previous.querySelector('.bc-profile-link')?.getAttribute('href');
  return `<article class="bc-provider-card" id="${id}" data-compliance-role="${roles}" data-search="${esc(`${name} ${roles.split(' ').map(r=>complianceRoles[r]).join(' ')} ${description} ${question}`.toLowerCase())}"><div class="bc-provider-top">${mark?.toString()||''}<div><p class="bc-company-index">${roles.split(' ').map(r=>complianceRoles[r]).join(' / ')}</p><h3>${esc(name)}</h3></div></div><p class="bc-desc">${esc(description)}</p><details class="bc-provider-details"><summary>Questions before shortlisting</summary><p>${esc(question)}</p></details>${profile?.startsWith('/fluidrwa/')?`<a class="bc-profile-link" href="${profile}">View company profile</a>`:`<a class="bc-profile-link" href="/submit-requirement?source=compliance-directory&amp;vendor=${encodeURIComponent(name)}">Discuss requirements</a>`}</article>`;
 }).join('');
 root.querySelector('main').set_content(`<section class="light-section-hero" id="compliance-buyer-guide"><div class="light-container"><p class="eyebrow light-eyebrow">Identity, transaction risk and compliance operations</p><h1>Crypto Compliance Software &amp; Infrastructure Providers</h1><p>A risk score is only useful when your team can explain the evidence and act on it. Compare the tools behind onboarding, screening, monitoring, Travel Rule communication and reporting.</p><p class="cc-updated">Buyer guide updated November 25, 2026</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn btn-primary light-primary" href="#vendor-directory">Explore 29 records A-Z</a><a class="btn btn-soft" href="#compliance-rfp">Prepare your brief</a></div><nav class="cc-jump" aria-label="Compliance buyer guide"><a href="#compliance-workflows">Choose the workflow</a><a href="#vendor-directory">Compare providers</a><a href="#compliance-coverage">Test the evidence</a><a href="#compliance-travel">Travel Rule exceptions</a><a href="#compliance-faq">FAQs</a></nav><p class="cc-note">For exchanges, custodians, payment teams, issuers and finance teams. Records include companies, products and an identity reference; they are not 29 independent or interchangeable AML vendors.</p></div></section>${section('vendor-directory','Directory A-Z','Compare the role before the brand',`<p class="cc-intro">Choose the operational function you need. Alphabetical order is not a performance ranking, and product records are identified separately from their supplier.</p><div class="bc-directory-head"></div><label class="bc-search"><span>Search tools and workflows</span><input id="bcSearch" type="search" placeholder="Company, monitoring, Travel Rule, tax or cases" autocomplete="off"></label><p class="bc-count" id="bcCount">Showing 29 records</p><div class="bc-provider-grid" id="bcGrid">${cards}</div>`)}${guide()}${section('compliance-faq','Questions buyers ask','Crypto compliance software FAQs',`<div class="bc-faq-list">${complianceFaq.map(([q,a])=>`<details class="bc-faq"><summary>${esc(q)}</summary><p>${esc(a)}</p></details>`).join('')}</div>`)}`);
 root.querySelector('script[type="application/ld+json"]').set_content(JSON.stringify({'@context':'https://schema.org','@graph':[
  {'@type':'CollectionPage','@id':`${complianceUrl}#webpage`,url:complianceUrl,name:complianceTitle,description:complianceDescription,dateModified:'2026-11-25',mainEntity:{'@id':`${complianceUrl}#providers`},citation:[...new Set(complianceRecords.map(r=>r[5]))]},
  {'@type':'BreadcrumbList',itemListElement:[{'@type':'ListItem',position:1,name:'Home',item:'https://www.fluidrwa.com/'},{'@type':'ListItem',position:2,name:'Web3 Vendors',item:'https://www.fluidrwa.com/web3vendorecosystem'},{'@type':'ListItem',position:3,name:'Compliance infrastructure',item:complianceUrl}]},
  {'@type':'ItemList','@id':`${complianceUrl}#providers`,numberOfItems:sorted.length,itemListElement:sorted.map(([id,name],i)=>({'@type':'ListItem',position:i+1,name,url:`${complianceUrl}#${id}`}))},
  {'@type':'FAQPage',mainEntity:complianceFaq.map(([name,text])=>({'@type':'Question',name,acceptedAnswer:{'@type':'Answer',text}}))}
 ]}));
 root.querySelector('#compliance-buyer-styles')?.remove();
 root.querySelector('head').insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend',`<style id="compliance-buyer-styles">#compliance-buyer-guide{padding:150px 0 32px;text-align:center}#compliance-buyer-guide h1{font-size:46px;line-height:1.15;max-width:1050px;margin:12px auto 22px}#compliance-buyer-guide>.light-container>p{max-width:920px;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto}#compliance-buyer-guide .hero-actions{justify-content:center}.cc-updated,.cc-note{font-size:15px!important;margin:18px auto!important}.cc-jump{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center;gap:14px 26px;border-block:1px solid #cbd9e8;padding:20px 0;margin:26px 0}.cc-jump a{font-weight:700}.cc-section{padding:40px 0!important;scroll-margin-top:132px}.cc-section:nth-of-type(even){background:#fff}.cc-section:not(#vendor-directory)>.light-container{max-width:1000px}.cc-section>.light-container>p{margin:0 0 20px;text-align:left}.cc-heading{max-width:930px;margin:0 auto 28px;text-align:center}.cc-heading h2{font-size:32px;line-height:1.25}.cc-section h3{font-size:23px;line-height:1.35;margin:26px 0 12px}.cc-section p,.cc-section li{line-height:1.75}.cc-intro{max-width:920px;margin:0 auto 24px!important;text-align:center!important}.cc-table{overflow-x:auto;border:1px solid #cbd9e8;border-radius:6px;margin:24px 0}.cc-table:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #1769b1}.cc-table table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:15px}.cc-table caption{padding:16px;background:#edf5fb;text-align:left;font-weight:700}.cc-table th,.cc-table td{padding:15px;min-width:175px;border-bottom:1px solid #d7e2ed;vertical-align:top;text-align:left}.cc-table thead th{background:#18486b;color:#fff}.cc-table tbody th{background:#f0f6fa;color:#152942}.cc-table tbody td{background:#fff;color:#25354b}.cc-actions{border-top:2px solid #d7b54d;margin-top:28px;padding-top:18px;text-align:center}.cc-actions>a{display:inline-block;margin:12px}.cc-section a{text-underline-offset:3px}.cc-section .bc-provider-card{min-width:0;border-radius:8px;box-shadow:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column}.cc-section .bc-provider-top{align-items:center}.cc-section .bc-provider-card h3{font-size:23px;overflow-wrap:anywhere;margin:8px 0}.cc-section .bc-company-index{line-height:1.5}.cc-section .bc-logo-mark{width:76px;height:76px;flex:0 0 76px}.cc-section .bc-provider-card details{margin-top:auto}#vendor-directory .bc-directory-head{display:block!important}#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-panel{padding:24px;gap:16px 24px;border-radius:8px;background:#fff;box-shadow:none;backdrop-filter:none;min-height:0}#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-panel:after{display:none}#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-intro{display:none}#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-search{grid-column:1}#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-filters{grid-column:2;border:0;padding:0;margin:0;align-items:end}#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-actions{grid-column:1/-1;justify-content:flex-start;flex-wrap:wrap}#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-actions a{border-radius:6px;background:#edf5fb;color:#155fa7;box-shadow:none;padding:10px 16px}#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-actions small{max-width:none;text-align:left;color:#52667a;font-size:13px}@media(max-width:700px){#compliance-buyer-guide{padding:120px 0 26px}#compliance-buyer-guide h1{font-size:34px}.cc-heading h2{font-size:27px}.cc-section{padding:28px 0!important;scroll-margin-top:110px}.cc-table th,.cc-table td{min-width:160px;padding:12px}.cc-section .bc-provider-top{align-items:flex-start}#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-panel{grid-template-columns:1fr;padding:18px}#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-search,#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-filters{grid-column:1}#vendor-directory .vendor-procurement-actions small{flex-basis:100%}}</style>`);
 return root.toString();
}
